# MZ Logistics Admin & Operations Portal - React Rules

Follow the global MZ Logistics rules.

This is a professional web administration and operations system.

Technology:
- React
- TypeScript
- Vite

## TypeScript

Use strict typing.

Avoid `any` unless absolutely unavoidable and documented.

Define proper:
- Interfaces.
- Types.
- API response types.
- Form models.

## Architecture

Use feature-based structure.

Example:

src/
  app/
  core/
  shared/
  features/
    dashboard/
    customers/
    service-providers/
    fleet/
    drivers/
    shipments/
    quotations/
    jobs/
    trips/
    tracking/
    payments/
    invoices/
    settlements/
    reports/
    users/
    roles/
    permissions/

Do not place all components in one components folder.

## Components

Create reusable components for repeated UI patterns.

Examples:
- DataTable
- StatusBadge
- PageHeader
- FilterBar
- SearchInput
- EmptyState
- LoadingState
- ConfirmDialog
- FormField
- DateRangeFilter

Do not over-abstract components used only once.

## Admin Design

The portal should feel like a premium enterprise logistics system.

Use:

- Professional sidebar.
- Clear top navigation.
- Structured dashboards.
- Clean data tables.
- Advanced filters.
- Search.
- Pagination.
- Status badges.
- Useful KPIs.
- Clear details pages.

Avoid generic AI dashboard designs.

Do not fill the dashboard with meaningless cards.

Every metric must serve an operational or business purpose.

## Tables

Admin relies heavily on tables.

Tables must support where relevant:

- Pagination.
- Sorting.
- Filtering.
- Search.
- Empty states.
- Loading states.
- Responsive behavior.
- Clear actions.

Do not show excessive row actions.

Use action menus when appropriate.

## RTL / LTR

Arabic:
- Full RTL.

English:
- Full LTR.

Sidebar direction must switch correctly.

Icons such as arrows and chevrons must respond to direction.

Tables and forms must remain readable in both languages.

## Roles and Permissions

Admin UI must support permission-based access.

Possible roles:

Super Admin:
- Full system access.

Operations:
- Shipment Requests.
- Quotations.
- Jobs.
- Trips.
- Tracking.

Finance:
- Payments.
- Invoices.
- Settlements.

Customer Support:
- Customers.
- Jobs.
- Trips.
- Support-related information.

Provider Management:
- Service Providers.
- Verification.
- Fleet-related review.

Auditor:
- Read-only access to permitted reports and audit information.

Do not authorize actions only in frontend.

Frontend permissions are for UI visibility and UX.

Backend authorization is mandatory.

## Forms

For long administrative forms:
- Group related fields.
- Use sections/tabs where appropriate.
- Keep validation clear.
- Avoid extremely long unstructured forms.

## Data Fetching

Centralize API calls.

Use proper caching and invalidation strategy where appropriate.

Avoid refetching everything after every small action.

## Error Handling

Provide clear user-facing errors.

Never expose raw server error messages or stack traces.

## Performance

Use:
- Code splitting where useful.
- Lazy-loaded routes where appropriate.
- Pagination.
- Memoization only when useful.

Do not prematurely optimize simple components.